+## Subtopics
+
+### AAA-Gaussians
+A method that eliminates sorting analytically by pre-integrating the alpha blending equation to compute an equivalent order-independent transparency through a rational polynomial approximation of the visibility term, suppressing popping artifacts without per-pixel depth sorting [S2, unofficial].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Drastically reduces the overhead of global radix sorting compared to vanilla 3DGS because sorting is performed on the smaller population of cell proxies rather than individual Gaussians [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduced memory consumption due to the smaller number of cells compared to Gaussians [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Enables real-time performance on diverse real-world datasets [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Eliminates popping artifacts (locally order-independent) and transparency artifacts (physically grounded transmittance) [S1]. Allows early termination for acceleration [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Order confusion caused by overlap among cells is a potential issue, addressed by the calibration scalar $v_n$ [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When real-time rendering of Gaussian Splatting is required with high visual fidelity, specifically avoiding transparency and popping artifacts common in sort-free OIT methods [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Reduced sorting overhead compared to standard 3DGS because sorting is performed on $N$ cells rather than $M$ Gaussians ($N \ll M$) [S1]. Early termination further accelerates rendering in opaque regions [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Lower memory consumption for sorting buffers due to the smaller number of cell proxies [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Eliminates transparency artifacts and popping artifacts common in vanilla OIT and sort-free methods [S1]. Enables real-time performance on resource-constrained devices [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires additional MLPs for decoding Gaussian attributes from cell features [S1]. Calibration scalar $v_n$ adds learnable parameters [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When real-time order-independent rendering is required with high visual fidelity, avoiding transparency artifacts [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Duplex-GS was introduced in the paper "Duplex-GS: Proxy-Guided Weighted Blending for Real-Time Order-Independent Gaussian Splatting" [S1]. It builds upon Scaffold-GS which introduced anchors as local encoding proxies [S1]. Duplex-GS replaces point anchors with ellipsoidal cells to provide explicit geometric structure [S1]. It addresses limitations of vanilla OIT-based Gaussian Splatting which lacks physical constraints and causes transparency artifacts [S1].
+Duplex-GS was introduced as a dual-hierarchy framework to address limitations in neural Gaussian frameworks and vanilla OIT-based Gaussian Splatting [S1]. It builds on Scaffold-GS, which uses anchors to emit neural Gaussians [S1]. The method was submitted to IEEE for publication in 2026 [S1].
 
